    <item>
      <title>PowerShell Profiles Explained (with Examples)</title>
      <link>https://diecknet.de/en/2024/03/05/powershell-profiles/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 05 Mar 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <author>Andreas Dieckmann</author>
      <guid>https://diecknet.de/en/2024/03/05/powershell-profiles/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;PowerShell profiles let you customize your PowerShell. For example, so it looks like this:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://diecknet.de/images/2024/2024-03-05_PSProfiles_OhMyPosh.jpg&#34; &gt;&lt;img loading=&#34;lazy&#34; src=&#34;https://diecknet.de/images/2024/2024-03-05_PSProfiles_OhMyPosh.jpg&#34; alt=&#34;PowerShell console customized with Oh My Posh and the Blue Owl theme&#34;  title=&#34;PowerShell console customized with Oh My Posh and the Blue Owl theme&#34;  /&gt;
&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Or you can configure it to show a splash screen at startup, such as with Winfetch:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://diecknet.de/images/2024/2024-03-05_PSProfiles_Winfetch.jpg&#34; &gt;&lt;img loading=&#34;lazy&#34; src=&#34;https://diecknet.de/images/2024/2024-03-05_PSProfiles_Winfetch.jpg&#34; alt=&#34;Example of a Winfetch information screen&#34;  title=&#34;Example of a Winfetch information screen&#34;  /&gt;
&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Or you can also store your own functions and variables so they are immediately available every time you start PowerShell.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
